WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.080
Not many remember the name of Mary Mallon.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:07.560
If not for tragic circumstances, a strange
immunity, and her position at the center of

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.980
an outbreak of disease, it's likely that she
would have faded into obscurity as just another

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:19.440
ordinary immigrant who gave up her homeland
for the promise of America in the mid-1880s.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.930
Mallon was just a teenager when she left Ireland
and headed west, landing in New York City

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.280
in 1883 or 1884.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.150
She had connections there, at least, an aunt
and uncle she lived with until she found her

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.150
feet.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.399
If that was all there was to the story, history
wouldn't remember her.

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:39.660
She would have been one of millions of Irish
who emigrated to the US in the 19th century,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:45.219
a time when an estimated 50 percent of the
country's population picked up roots to try

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:46.940
to make a better life elsewhere.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:52.339
But Mallon was carrying a deadly disease with
her, and marked her as Typhoid Mary.

00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:56.589
But there's more to her story than just a
gory nickname, and remembering her as just

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:01.309
as a carrier of disease doesn't quite do her
credit.

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:12.490
TITLE: What is typhoid fever, anyway?

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:17.160
Since typhoid fever is one of those olde-timey
diseases that isn't talked about much in the

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:22.340
modern, industrialized world, this conversation
deserves a short foreword on what exactly

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.520
the disease is.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.440
It's caused by the bacteria Salmonella typhi,
and most of the time, it's passed from one

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.260
person to the next through food or water that's
been contaminated.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.860
In some cases, being in close contact with
someone that's infected can spread the disease,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:38.860
too.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.040
Symptoms can take up to a week to develop,
and they're generally unpleasant.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:49.000
The infected have to contend with a wide range
of disease markers, including fatigue, muscle

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.440
aches, abdominal pains, swelling, and sweating,
ranging all the way up to fever and extreme

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.700
gastrointestinal distress.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.610
Without treatment, the disease can progress
to delirium and the so-called typhoid state:

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.690
laying there, too exhausted to move, eyes
half-open.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:09.819
It's at that stage when other complications
can develop: some might start suffering from

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:16.270
hallucinations and paranoid psychosis; others
might develop inflammation of the heart, pancreas,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:17.270
or brain.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:22.110
The most serious complication is intestinal
bleeding, which, in some cases, can lead to

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.660
holes in the intestines.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.730
Typhoid is treatable today, but even modern
vaccinations aren't 100 percent effective.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:33.650
The cause of typhoid was confirmed in 1880,
after years of attempts to document exactly

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:38.440
how contained instances of disease can turn
into epidemics.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.700
Typhoid had about a 10 percent fatality rate
at the turn of the 20th century, and the first

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.400
vaccine wasn't developed until 1911.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:51.459
That's five years after Mary Mallon was hired
to cook for a well-to-do New York banker named

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:55.900
Charles Henry Warren and his family.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.920
TITLE: Typhoid Summer

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.990
Not much is known about Mallon's early life
in Ireland, or her first years in the US.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:09.370
She was born in County Tyrone — one of the
poorest areas of Northern Ireland — on September

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:15.910
23, 1869, and left her homeland for the US
when she was only 14 or 15 years old.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.710
There was nothing unusual about her; in fact,
she followed the normal route of settling

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:25.820
in with family members who had made the crossing
before her, then finding work as a domestic

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:26.820
servant.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:31.150
Things started to change in 1900, when one
of her employers discovered she was a talented

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:32.150
cook.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.790
They put her to work in the kitchens, and
it was a move that changed her life, and the

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.000
lives of those who employed her.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.760
Mallon worked for eight families in the years
between 1900 and 1907, and where she went,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.130
typhoid fever often followed.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:51.220
At the time, typhoid was an illness associated
with crowded and poor urban communities with

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:55.160
nonexistent sanitation and little access to
fresh drinking water.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.701
Mallon, however, was working for some of the
most affluent families in the city, which

00:03:58.701 --> 00:04:03.230
were not exactly the sort of families that
tended not to suffer from afflictions like

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:04.349
typhoid.

00:04:04.349 --> 00:04:09.360
Doctors did notice they were unusual victims,
but remained baffled by the outbreak of illness

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.569
in homes where there seemed to be no logical
explanation for it.

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:18.019
Mallon’s peers became sick at seven of the
eight jobs she held in those years.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:21.519
At least 22 individuals fell ill, and at least
one died.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:26.040
There's nothing that indicates Mallon initially
had any idea the illnesses were connected

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:30.100
to her in any way, and she continued to cook
for various families.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.220
Because she did move around a bit, and because
she never left a forwarding address, it took

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.490
a while before anyone could connect the dots.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:39.770
In the summer of 1906, Mallon was hired to
be a summer cook for Charles Henry Warren

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:40.770
and his family.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:44.940
They had rented a house on Long Island and
in August, one of his daughters took ill.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.230
It was, of course, Typhoid, and it wasn't
long before her sister, mother, a gardener,

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.210
and two maids were also sick.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:56.280
The owner of the house was George Thompson,
and he was understandably worried; no one

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.650
would want to vacation there if it gained
a reputation as a place where an entire family

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.590
could fall mysteriously ill.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:07.060
They were afraid the area water source was
contaminated, and knew they had to find out

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.210
exactly what happened before they could rent
the property again.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.440
Thompson looked for and found a professional
to get to the bottom of things.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.840
He hired New York City Department of Health
sanitary engineer George Soper to find the

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.970
source of infection, and he knew exactly what
to look for, as he had specialized in the

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.300
study of typhoid outbreaks.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.520
At first, Soper thought the freshwater clams
that had been served to the family were a

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.860
likely source, but not everyone who developed
typhoid had eaten them.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.120
Eventually, he honed in on Mallon as another
potential source for the disease.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.010
His theory was quickly confirmed.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:43.190
Mallon moved on from the Warren family and
showed up next as a cook in a home on Manhattan's

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:44.510
Park Avenue.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:49.729
That's where Soper followed her, and when
he met her.

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:53.680
TITLE: Confronting Typhoid Mary

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.400
In order to confirm Mary Mallon was, indeed,
a carrier of typhoid, Soper knew he needed

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.800
to have her bodily fluids tested for presence
of the bacteria.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.170
It's hard to imagine just how awkward that
conversation had to be; when Soper introduced

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.520
himself to her and asked her to provide samples,
well, he described the encounter like this:

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.889
"I was as diplomatic as possible, but I had
to say I suspected her of making people sick

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:17.180
and that I wanted specimens of her urine,
feces, and blood.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.270
It did not take Mary long to react to this
suggestion.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.010
She seized a carving fork and advanced in
my direction.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:28.090
I passed rapidly down the long narrow hall,
through the tall, iron gate, and so to the

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:29.090
sidewalk.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.560
I felt rather lucky to escape."

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.240
Now, with the benefit of hindsight, it's easy
to vilify Mallon.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.990
We know she was a carrier of Typhoid, but
she almost certainly did not.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:43.440
She had never developed the disease herself,
and she did, at one point, voluntarily subject

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.370
herself to testing for the bacteria.

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:49.370
The chemist who performed the tests — who
was a respected professional — declared

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:51.230
she was free of any sign of disease.

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:56.160
It's suspected the tests were performed when
she was in a period of remission, which certainly

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.390
didn't make things any easier on either her
or Soper.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:05.139
Just imagine: a stranger appears out of nowhere
and accuses you of being responsible for spreading

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:07.430
disease and sickness everywhere you've worked.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:11.850
You are, allegedly, responsible for deaths,
despite the fact that you feel fine and have

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:12.850
never been sick.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:15.990
At one point, a doctor has even told you that
you're fine.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:21.210
It's no wonder that Mallon was less than willing
to submit to more tests, amid rumors that

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:23.980
would put her entire livelihood on the line.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:28.639
There is another element at work here, too,
that may have attributed to Mallon's violent

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:30.520
outbursts when confronted.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:36.020
The late 1800s was a time when the Irish were
emigrating to the US in droves, fleeing oppression

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.059
and starvation in their homeland.

00:07:38.059 --> 00:07:43.080
Some made the journey on so-called "coffin
ships," packed with desperate immigrants and

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:48.350
so filled with disease and death that an estimated
25 percent died before they completed their

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:49.350
journey.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:51.600
Their bodies ended up at the bottom of the
Atlantic.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.040
Those that did reach America's shores were
starving and destitute by the time they got

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:58.190
there, and there were a lot of them.

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:01.740
Anti-Irish and anti-Catholic sentiment was
strong, and while there were some groups who

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:07.140
offered charity to the refuges, there were
more that whispered of a secret plot orchestrated

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:12.160
by the Vatican to instill Catholicism in America
as the dominant religion.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:18.000
The Irish were condemned for supposedly taking
jobs from Americans and gallivanting as drunken

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.000
criminals.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.520
Politicians who railed about the Irish nuisance
were preaching loudly from their pulpits to

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.330
a public that was more than happy to listen.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:29.210
Homes and churches were burnt, and mob violence
took lives.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:34.000
It was only in the 1880s that some politicians
began to see the large numbers of Irish as

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:40.250
valuable allies, but signs of "No Irish Need
Apply" were still fresh in everyone's memory.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:45.500
For Mallon, it very well could have felt that
this was just one more way her new home was

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.780
persecuting her for the accent she kept throughout
her entire life.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:49.780
Soper couldn’t give up, though, as he was
sure he was right.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:52.710
Still, Soper knew he was right and couldn't
walk away.

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:57.510
He discovered exactly what it was that Mallon
had served to get everyone sick: ice cream

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.040
with fresh peaches.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.570
Heat may have destroyed the bacteria in other
foods, but the uncooked peaches and Mallon's

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:06.910
unwashed hands were the perfect way for disease
to spread.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:15.430
In 1906, there were 3,467 reported cases of
typhoid, and 639 of them were deadly.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:19.339
The actual number of cases was probably much
higher, and if Typhoid Mary was spreading

00:09:19.339 --> 00:09:23.570
it everywhere she went, it was only a matter
of time before the city had a potential outbreak

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:24.570
on its hands.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:29.459
At the time, Soper was familiar with the idea
that people infected with disease could pass

00:09:29.459 --> 00:09:33.000
bacteria on to others through various bodily
fluids.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.940
But since Mallon wasn't sick and never had
been, this was completely different from anything

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:39.260
he'd seen before.

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:44.029
By the time Soper caught up to her at her
new job in Manhattan, there were already instances

00:09:44.029 --> 00:09:48.680
of typhoid in the household she was cooking
for; one servant was infected, and the family's

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.500
only daughter was dying.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:55.000
So, he followed her to the boarding house
she was living in and confronted her again,

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:56.930
with no more luck than the last time.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:02.010
After he was turned down for fluid donations
a second time, Soper requested help from the

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.140
New York City Health Department.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:07.230
He gave them a warning: in this case, they
needed to be prepared to use force.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:11.220
The Health Department gave her one more chance
to peacefully agree to submit to testing,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.560
and sent Dr. S. Josephine Baker to see her.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.010
When she still refused to cooperate, they
sent the police.

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:22.040
Mallon tried to flee, but was eventually found,
forced into an ambulance, and taken to the

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:27.110
Willard Parker Hospital in what Dr. Baker
described as a "wild" ride, one where Dr.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:30.780
Baker was forced to sit on her the entire
way to help restrain her.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.290
She wrote, "...it was like being in a cage
with an angry lion," and it was no wonder

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:36.290
she struggled.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:41.070
Police had carried her into the ambulance
after a five-hour chase, and Mallon was convinced

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.339
she had done nothing wrong.

00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:43.339
TITLE: Mary Mallon enters into medical history

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:47.149
Once Mallon was transferred to the care of
a hospital, her bodily fluids were tested

00:10:47.149 --> 00:10:51.170
repeatedly — three times a week, from March
to November — for traces of the bacteria

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:52.890
that causes typhoid.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:57.500
Most of the tests confirmed the Typhoid bacteria
was there, but there were still some tests

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:01.960
that came back negative, which explains why
she might have had a clean test prior to Soper's

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:02.960
intervention.

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.810
It wasn't long before Soper found himself
sympathizing with Mallon.

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:08.420
She hadn't infected people on purpose, after
all, but she was still dangerous.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:13.440
And now, she was being held in a small, uncomfortable
hospital room, under lock and key, while she

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.540
herself was of perfectly sound mind and body.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:20.850
He tried talking to her, getting her to cooperate,
even suggesting she could have her gallbladder

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:24.770
removed, as it was a nonessential organ that
was probably the source of the infection.

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:26.150
But she refused to talk to him.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:31.800
She stayed in custody for a full two years
before she sued for her release.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:35.580
Mallon argued in court that she had never
been sick, but the courts viewed the findings

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:39.180
of the medical research far more compelling,
and refused to release her.

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:43.089
That was, they said, largely because they
didn't want to bear any responsibility, should

00:11:43.089 --> 00:11:45.790
anyone else get sick or die from contact with
her.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:49.670
It was another eleven months before Mallon
made a deal with the Health Department: she

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:53.529
could go free as long as she checked in with
them every three months, and never cooked

00:11:53.529 --> 00:11:55.899
or handled food for others again.

00:11:55.899 --> 00:12:00.380
Mallon agreed, was released, and promptly
disappeared.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.390
Here's where things get a little dicey.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:06.779
Mallon did spend some time working in other
professions, and she did get jobs in places

00:12:06.779 --> 00:12:09.730
like laundries, where she would have been
no danger.

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:13.730
But she found it almost impossible to support
herself, and the temptation to return to cooking

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.620
— which paid a lot more — was too high.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:20.600
So she went back in the kitchen, where she
continued to cook and continued to infect

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:22.200
a lot of people.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.329
Soper was officially off the case, but he
continued to keep an eye on her.

00:12:25.329 --> 00:12:29.850
She popped up cooking under the pseudonyms
Mrs. Brown and Marie Breshof, finding work

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:34.730
in a fancy hotel and a sanitorium, both in
New Jersey, along with a hotel in Southampton

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:36.550
and a restaurant on Broadway.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:40.610
It's impossible to tell just how many cases
Mallon caused, but there were a lot.

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:44.700
It was during this period that the press had
officially christened her Typhoid Mary.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:45.990
But here's the thing.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:50.340
There's no real evidence that suggests Mallon
ever truly believed that she was the cause

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:55.800
of all these instances of typhoid fever at
the time she was first arrested and diagnosed.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:13:00.959
She was the very first example of a completely
asymptomatic patient ever studied.

00:13:00.959 --> 00:13:05.140
If the medical community didn't understand
the mechanics of what was going on, how was

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:06.529
Mallon supposed to?

00:13:06.529 --> 00:13:10.390
It's also suggested that there was another
massive oversight in Mallon’s case.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:13.790
When she was released, she was told not to
cook, and to take extra care when it came

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:14.790
to hygiene.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:19.649
But she was never given any sort of specific
instruction on how to properly disinfect herself

00:13:19.649 --> 00:13:24.230
and keep the bacteria off her hands, and she
was never given any kind of retraining or

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:26.649
direction on other ways she could support
herself.

00:13:26.649 --> 00:13:29.589
There was no welfare system or aid available.

00:13:29.589 --> 00:13:33.760
Mallon assumed authorities were simply trying
to label her a scapegoat for the outbreaks;

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:39.220
she didn't seem to think she was actually
doing any harm when she returned to cooking.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:43.899
TITLE: The Question of Confinement

00:13:43.899 --> 00:13:47.850
Mallon was initially released in 1910, and
it wasn't until five years later that a man

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.959
named Dr. Edward B. Cragin approached Soper.

00:13:50.959 --> 00:13:55.639
He was an obstetrician at the Sloane Hospital
for Women, a facility that had recently had

00:13:55.639 --> 00:13:58.190
more than 20 people fall ill to typhoid.

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:03.040
He had a suspect and a sample of her handwriting,
and asked Soper if he could identify their

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:04.040
cook.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:05.040
He could.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:06.040
It was Mallon.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:09.250
Soper advised him to reach out to the Health
Department, and he did.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:13.209
Mallon was arrested and sent to quarantine
on North Brother Island, an island in the

00:14:13.209 --> 00:14:17.890
East River that was home to a hospital and
quarantine facility built in 1885.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:22.540
It was home to patients suffering from illnesses
like tuberculosis, smallpox, yellow fever,

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:24.550
and, of course, typhoid.

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.770
Mallon would live among them until her death
in 1938.

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:31.970
Instead of feeling relief, Soper painted a
heartbreaking picture of a woman who no longer

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:33.500
fought against her own confinement.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:34.500
He wrote:

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.610
"...she had been advertised to the world as
a dangerous person and had been treated worse

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:44.649
than a criminal, and yet she had not been
guilty of the least violence toward anybody."

00:14:44.649 --> 00:14:48.649
Mallon seemed resigned to her lot in life,
finally admitting to herself that there was

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:52.550
a wave of typhoid that swept along behind
her wherever she went.

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:57.649
She was not the same person when she was apprehended
the second time, and there was little left

00:14:57.649 --> 00:15:01.000
of what Soper described as her "almost pathological
anger."

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:05.490
She settled into her confinement willingly
enough, and they gave her a job in the laboratory,

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:06.850
where she ran medical tests.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:10.339
They even gave her permission to visit the
mainland, where she regularly headed into

00:15:10.339 --> 00:15:11.339
the city.

00:15:11.339 --> 00:15:12.519
She always came back.

00:15:12.519 --> 00:15:17.720
If she seemed resigned to her later confinement,
it was a resignation she hadn't felt in her

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.050
younger years.

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:23.389
Most of what we know of Mallon is through
the writings of Soper and other medical professionals,

00:15:23.389 --> 00:15:26.550
although there are a few of her letters that
have survived.

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:31.050
In one, she describes being so distraught
over being arrested and confined to a hospital

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:35.430
for the first time, that she developed an
eye twitch that led to the paralysis of one

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:37.940
eye — a condition which went untreated.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:42.029
When she was given medication, she complained
that she didn't need it: it was mainly used

00:15:42.029 --> 00:15:45.660
to treat kidney issues, and she didn't suffer
from those.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:50.100
She wrote of doctors that told her she needed
to have her gallbladder removed, then admitted

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:52.579
to her that the surgery might not make a difference.

00:15:52.579 --> 00:15:55.180
And she wrote: "I'm a little afraid of the
people…

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.260
I have been in fact a peep show for everybody.

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:02.110
Even the interns had to come to see me and
ask about the facts already known to the whole

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:03.380
wide world.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:07.040
The tuberculosis men would say, "There she
is, the kidnapped woman."

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:08.769
Dr. Park has had me illustrated in Chicago.

00:16:08.769 --> 00:16:13.720
I wonder how the said Dr. William Park would
like to be insulted and put in the Journal

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:17.190
and call him or his wife Typhoid William Park."

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:22.230
It brings up an important question: did Mallon
deserve to be vilified to such an extreme

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:23.230
extent?

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:27.350
Should she have been advertised as cracking
skulls into a frying pan, as at least one

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:29.130
illustration showed her doing?

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:34.880
Or was she simply a victim of typhoid in her
own way, too?

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:39.040
TITLE: Unraveling the Mystery of Contagion

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:43.320
Mary Mallon was credited with directing spreading
typhoid to at least 50 people, and three of

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:44.329
them died.

00:16:44.329 --> 00:16:49.800
She's even been named as the Patient Zero
who likely started a typhoid outbreak in 1907,

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:53.050
one that ultimately escalated to around 3,000
cases.

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:56.590
Even though she's the one most infamous when
it comes to healthy people who have had the

00:16:56.590 --> 00:17:00.480
potential to spread a deadly disease, she
was far from the only one.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:04.309
By the time she died, more than 400 others
had been identified in New York alone.

00:17:04.309 --> 00:17:08.529
In fact, somewhere between one and six percent
of those infected with the type of salmonella

00:17:08.529 --> 00:17:13.289
that causes typhoid become healthy carriers,
and it's a huge problem the medical world

00:17:13.289 --> 00:17:15.669
hasn't known too much about until recently.

00:17:15.669 --> 00:17:20.089
It wasn't until 2013 that studies done by
Stanford University School of Medicine researcher

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:25.619
Denise Monack yielded some insight into demonstrating
the mechanisms by which bacteria might infect

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:27.939
a person without making them ill.

00:17:27.939 --> 00:17:31.789
Figuring out the mechanics of just how it
all works could lead to the development of

00:17:31.789 --> 00:17:37.299
a treatment that would block typhoid's ability
to hitch a ride in a seemingly healthy host,

00:17:37.299 --> 00:17:38.529
just as it did to Mallon.

00:17:38.529 --> 00:17:40.779
Today, there are a lot like her out there.

00:17:40.779 --> 00:17:45.379
Typhoid impacts around 16 million people a
year across the globe, and even on the low

00:17:45.379 --> 00:17:51.289
end, that's 160,000 people capable of spreading
infection just like Mallon did.

00:17:51.289 --> 00:17:55.239
Typhoid Mary died alone.

00:17:55.239 --> 00:18:00.989
Soper wrote about her death after he was fairly
certain he had tracked down her sister, though

00:18:00.989 --> 00:18:03.559
he could never conclusively prove their relation.

00:18:03.559 --> 00:18:08.739
On Christmas morning in 1932, she was discovered
on the floor of her cottage, paralyzed from

00:18:08.739 --> 00:18:09.739
a stroke.

00:18:09.739 --> 00:18:14.340
She was moved to the hospital, where she lived
until her death on November 11, 1938.

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:18.149
Afterwards, there were advertisements taken
out in several newspapers for the span of

00:18:18.149 --> 00:18:22.759
a month, appealing for relatives to come forward
to claim the little money she left behind.

00:18:22.759 --> 00:18:23.899
No one ever did.

00:18:23.899 --> 00:18:28.269
Only nine people attended her funeral, and
no one followed her to witness her burial

00:18:28.269 --> 00:18:30.509
at St. Raymond's Cemetery in the Bronx.

00:18:30.509 --> 00:18:33.259
There's a bit of rumor surrounding her death
as well.

00:18:33.259 --> 00:18:38.090
An autopsy was said to have turned up a high
concentration of bacteria in her gallbladder.

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.419
If she had agreed to the operation to remove
it, would she have been cured?

00:18:40.419 --> 00:18:42.090
It's entirely possible, the rumor implies,
but it's not that clear-cut a case of regret.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:46.509
Some researchers — including Soper — say
there never was an autopsy, and the tale is

00:18:46.509 --> 00:19:02.519
retold to help justify her imprisonment.

00:19:02.519 --> 00:19:10.929
Which is true?

00:19:10.929 --> 00:19:16.070
It's impossible to say.

